# Dropdown Menu & Overflow Menu

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

A dropdown lets someone choose one item from a list that is too long to show in full. An overflow menu holds the less common actions for an item, behind a three-dot button. Home Assistant uses a native select, its own select, a dropdown and an overflow menu for these jobs.

Think first whether a shorter question or a visible group of [radios](radios.html) would be clearer. A select suits a long, familiar list where the choice can be undone, such as the seven Civica theme colours.

This page covers:

- Dropdown Menu & Overflow Menu
  - `<select>`
    - `<optgroup>`
    - `<option>`
  - `ha-select`
  - `ha-dropdown`
  - `ha-button-menu`
  - `ha-icon-overflow-menu`
  - `ha-check-list-item`
  - `ha-clickable-list-item`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

A dropdown has a field that shows the current choice and a list that opens from it. A label sits above the field, with an optional hint and, when needed, an error message. In Home Assistant’s own select the field is a button, and the list opens below it.

An overflow menu has a three-dot button and a list of actions that opens from it. Each action has a label and sometimes an icon. A risky action is marked and sits last.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

The Civica theme sets `primary-color` (`#1D70B8` in light mode, `#C0D7EB` in dark mode), `card-background-color` (`#FFFFFF`, `#24282B`, used for the menu surface), `primary-text-color`, `secondary-text-color`, `divider-color` (`#CECECE`, `#50565A`) and `disabled-text-color`. A selected item uses `primary-color`, with a pale tint that Home Assistant sets itself. The theme sets nothing for popup placement or animation.

<h3 id="child-select"><code>&lt;select&gt;</code></h3>

The native select. It has a visible label, a name and, optionally, `required`, `disabled` or `multiple`. Without `multiple` it is a dropdown. The keyboard works as the browser provides: arrow keys, typing to jump to a match, Home and End, and a key to open the list. The closed field can be styled. The open list belongs to the browser or phone, so do not expect it to follow the theme.

<h3 id="child-select--optgroup"><code>&lt;optgroup&gt;</code></h3>

A named group of options inside a select. The group name is a heading that cannot be chosen. Groups cannot be nested. Use one only when the list is long and has natural groups, such as rooms by floor.

<h3 id="child-select--option"><code>&lt;option&gt;</code></h3>

One choice. Its text is what people see. In a single select, the first option is chosen by default unless one is marked as selected. A required select that has not been answered should start with a placeholder option, such as “Choose a dashboard”, so the first real option is not chosen by accident.

<h3 id="child-ha-select"><code>ha-select</code></h3>

Home Assistant’s own select. A dashboard author does not write one; you meet it in settings dialogs and forms. It has a label, an optional helper line, a current value, and can be required, disabled or clearable. The field is a button with the label as its accessible name, and the choices open in a dropdown below it, sized to the field. The chosen item is marked. When the select is disabled, the list does not open.

<h3 id="child-ha-dropdown"><code>ha-dropdown</code></h3>

Home Assistant’s popup menu. Any element in its trigger slot, often an icon button, opens it, and it holds dropdown items. Items can be plain, show a tick for a checked state, have an icon, be disabled or be marked as dangerous. The menu surface takes `card-background-color`. Menu rows have a minimum height set by Home Assistant. Keyboard use follows the underlying component.

<h3 id="child-ha-button-menu"><code>ha-button-menu</code></h3>

This Home Assistant version does not use the name `ha-button-menu`. Menus are built with the dropdown described above and its items, with any element as the trigger, such as an icon button. Use the dropdown above. Do not confuse it with the menu button in the header, which opens and closes the sidebar and is not a dropdown.

<h3 id="child-ha-icon-overflow-menu"><code>ha-icon-overflow-menu</code></h3>

A set of actions for one item, such as a row in a list. On a narrow screen it shows one three-dot button that opens a dropdown of the actions. On a wider screen it shows each action as its own labelled icon button, with a tooltip, separated by thin lines. Each action has an icon and a label. An action can be marked as a warning, shown in the dropdown as dangerous. If there are no actions, nothing is drawn.

The three-dot button takes its accessible name from a label, “Overflow menu” by default. Give it a name that says what it opens, such as “More actions for Evening lights”. Each icon button is named from its action’s label.

<h3 id="child-ha-check-list-item"><code>ha-check-list-item</code></h3>

A row with text and a checkbox, used in lists where several items can be chosen. It is not a dropdown. It can show a mixed state, and it can be set so that only the checkbox itself responds. The checkbox takes `primary-color`. Use it for choices such as which entities to include; for a single choice use a select or [radios](radios.html).

<h3 id="child-ha-clickable-list-item"><code>ha-clickable-list-item</code></h3>

This Home Assistant version does not use the name `ha-clickable-list-item`. A row you can select is the list item button, `ha-list-item-button`. It has a start area for an icon, a headline and an end area, and it can be a link. Home Assistant uses it for the sidebar rows. Give every such row a text label.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- The native select works with the keyboard, phone pickers and assistive technology without extra work.
- The native select’s open list cannot be themed, and Home Assistant’s dropdown colours come from the card colours, not from a setting of its own.
- The three-dot button hides actions, so people may not find them.
- Home Assistant’s dropdowns and overflow menus are drawn by the app. A dashboard author cannot add them.
- The old names `ha-button-menu` and `ha-clickable-list-item` are not used in this release.
- You cannot change the popup’s position, animation or hover colour.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Use a select for a long, familiar list with a reversible choice. Do not use it for two simple choices, and do not use a multi-select for rooms. Keep the native select instead of replacing it with a custom menu.

- Give a visible label and a useful hint. Use [hint text](hint-text.html) and [error messages](error-message.html) in the usual way.
- Preselect the saved choice when someone edits it, and keep its name in text. A colour swatch on its own does not name the choice.
- Do not use the first option as an accidental answer. A required question starts with a placeholder.
- Selecting an item must not send a device command or reset something else, such as the chosen Energy period. Do not submit the form when the choice changes.
- Never put the main action in an overflow menu. Show it as a visible button.
- Name the three-dot button for what it opens. Keep the menu short. Put risky items last, and say what they do in words, such as “Delete automation”, because colour alone does not.
- If you cannot show a hidden menu, show the actions as visible buttons.

Success criteria that apply:

- 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value: the trigger says what it opens.
- 1.3.1 and 3.3.2: a visible label and instructions.
- 3.3.1 Error Identification: an error says what to choose.
- 2.1.1 Keyboard, 2.4.3 Focus Order and 2.4.7 Focus Visible: the native select works with arrow keys and typing, and Home Assistant’s dropdown follows its underlying component.
- 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum): menu rows have a minimum height set by Home Assistant.
- 1.4.13 Content on Hover or Focus: tooltips on overflow icon buttons.
- 3.2.2 On Input: choosing an item does not trigger a surprise.
- 2.5.1 Pointer Gestures: every action has a simple tap.

Test arrow keys, typing a matching option, phone pickers, saved state and errors. See [Test the interface](../accessibility/testing.html).

